## Objective

Split the rule mote into its actual parts, and take the abstractions
that fall out of that split.

A rule mote today carries five distinct responsibilities smeared across
two motes and one expression syntax. This proposal gives each one an
ordinary, visible home in the graph, so that the rule mote keeps exactly
one thing -- **the mathematics** -- and everything it was implicitly
carrying becomes something the user can see, edit, and query.

The decomposition is not the goal on its own. Three of the four homes it
creates are abstractions MonOSS does not have and would clearly benefit
from: **roles** (a relation over several motes, not one), **freedoms**
(what a specific mote is free to have determined, not what its type is),
and **scenarios** (a named perturbation of the garden, so one graph can
hold more than one answer). The fifth home, **provenance**, is the thing
that makes any of it explicable.

This proposal deliberately does **not** adopt the directed-computation
architecture of `docs/modeling_architecture_proposal.md`. MonOSS solves
undirected constraint systems; a directed computation is the degenerate
case, not the foundation. See §9 for what is being declined and why.

---

## 0. Diagnosis: what a rule mote actually carries

A rule today is a mote with an `equation` property and an `applies_to`
relationship pointing at a species (`core/rules.py`). The species
separately carries an `unknowns` property. Between them, five different
things are being expressed:

| # | Responsibility | Where it lives now | Problem |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | **The statement.** `voltage == current * resistance` | `equation` on the rule mote | None. This part is right. |
| 2 | **Scope.** For which motes is it asserted? | `applies_to` edge to a species | Only ever "every instance of one species". A relation can't be asserted about one specific mote, or about a group. |
| 3 | **Binding.** How do names resolve to motes? | Baked into the *syntax*: bare names anchor on the current mote, `targets('a').v` navigates | Undeclared and unchecked. A mis-wired graph fails at solve time with "found 0 motes", not at check time. |
| 4 | **Freedom.** Which properties may the solver determine? | `unknowns` on the *species* | Named for the solver, not the model, and in the wrong mote entirely -- and at the wrong granularity: solvability is asserted about a *type*, when it is a fact about a particular *problem*. |
| 5 | **Provenance.** Why is the answer what it is? | Nowhere | `simulation_changes` records that a value changed, never which equation and which inputs produced it. |

Responsibility 4 is the sharpest smell: the rule states the relation but
a *different mote* decides what the relation is allowed to determine.
Reading a rule tells you nothing about whether it will solve.

### The principle

> **The rule keeps the mathematics. Scope lives on the edge, binding
> lives in the graph, freedom lives on the mote that has it, and
> provenance lives in the log.**

Note what this does *not* require: no new object types, no `Computation`,
no tables for structure. Scope is already an edge. Binding is already a
relationship. Freedom is already a property. The work is moving three
things to where they belong and writing down the fourth.

---

## 1. The target decomposition

| Concern | Today | Proposed |
|---|---|---|
| Statement | `equation` on rule mote | unchanged |
| Scope | `applies_to` → species | `applies_to` → species **or a single mote**, inferred from the target |
| Binding | implicit anchor + inline `targets()` | **roles**: a relationship type, declared on the species, addressed as `role.property` |
| Freedom | `unknowns` on species | **`freedoms`** on the **mote**, defaulting to its species, overridable by a **scenario** |
| Provenance | -- | derived **derivation log** (§5) |

Every row either narrows the rule mote's job or gives an existing
implicit thing an explicit home. Nothing is added that isn't already
being expressed somewhere less visibly.

